  "title": "Iraq Says It’s Stopping Oil Smuggling. Why Is It Moving to Take Over Kurdish Oilfields?",
  "deck": "Baghdad ordered federal management of oilfields under Kurdish security control and moved to take over border crossings. Kurdish leaders pushed back, and Iraq’s top court affirmed federal authority over international borders.",
  "category": "world",
  "categoryLabel": "World",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-25T13:27:23Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-25T13:27:23Z",
  "body": [
    "Iraq’s prime minister says he is protecting the country’s oil wealth after an investigation into suspected smuggling. The orders he issued Tuesday reach beyond the state company at the center of that inquiry. They direct Baghdad’s oil ministry to assume management of fields under Kurdish security control in four provinces and direct federal authorities to take over international crossings in the Kurdistan Region. The region’s prime minister, Masrour Barzani, rejected the moves. [Reuters](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/iraqi-pm-orders-takeover-kurdistan-regions-border-crossings-after-oil-smuggling-2026-11-24/) · [The National](https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/mena/2026/11/24/iraqs-pm-orders-federal-takeover-of-kurdistan-oil-exports-border-crossings-and-oilfields-in-disputed-areas/)",
    "On Wednesday, Iraq’s Federal Supreme Court ruled that Baghdad can deploy federal forces along international borders, including in the Kurdistan Region, Iraqi state television reported. The ruling gives Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi legal backing on border security. The immediate question for the oil industry is whether federal officials actually assume management at the fields covered by his order. [Reuters on the ruling](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/iraqs-top-court-says-baghdad-can-deploy-troops-secure-borders-kurdistan-region-2026-11-25/) · [Rudaw](https://www.rudaw.net/english/categories/iraq/1080314)",
    "## How an oil probe became a fight over control",
    "Zaidi announced the measures after an inquiry involving the state-run North Oil Company. Iraq’s state news agency, citing what it described as a government intelligence report, said some oil produced by the company was diverted into a smuggling network. Reuters said it could not independently verify the report’s findings. Zaidi suspended the company’s director general and deputies pending further investigation, Reuters reported. [Reuters](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/iraqi-pm-orders-takeover-kurdistan-regions-border-crossings-after-oil-smuggling-2026-11-24/)",
    "The order applies to fields under Kurdish security control in disputed areas of Diyala, Kirkuk, Salahuddin and Nineveh. Zaidi also told the Kurdistan Regional Government to route approvals for crude and petroleum-product exports through the federal Oil Ministry. Kurdish regional President Nechirvan Barzani said the two governments were already in talks and called for resolving the dispute through the constitution and respect for the region’s authority. [The National](https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/mena/2026/11/24/iraqs-pm-orders-federal-takeover-of-kurdistan-oil-exports-border-crossings-and-oilfields-in-disputed-areas/) · [Kurdistan Region presidency](https://presidency.gov.krd/en/statement-by-president-nechirvan-barzani-1/)",
    "## Why the pipeline to Turkey matters",
    "Baghdad already oversees Kurdish crude exports through Turkey, and the proceeds go to the federal treasury, according to the region’s natural resources ministry, as reported by Rudaw. The immediate stakes are who manages the fields and controls access to export markets. A previous dispute halted pipeline shipments to Turkey for more than two years before the sides agreed to restart them in September 2025. [Rudaw](https://rudaw.net/english/categories/iraq/1080288) · [Reuters explainer](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/what-are-disputes-between-iraqs-federal-government-kurdistan-region-2026-11-24/)",
    "The next test is whether Baghdad and Erbil agree on how the orders will be carried out. A change in field operators, federal officials taking over a crossing, or a shift in pipeline volumes would show how far the directives have moved from paper to practice."
  ],
